  "notes": "West leads the ♣Q against 6♠. The lead of the ♣Q conventionally shows the ♣J. The only possible problem is a 3-0 trump break. In this case, South must hope to engineer an endplay to avoid his club loser.\n\nThe opening lead should be won with the ♣K so that 1 of dummy's hearts can be ruffed. South should next cash the ♠A, revealing the 3-0 trump break. A second trump should be played to the ♠K and dummy's remaining heart ruffed.\n\nSouth should now play diamonds. If West refuses to ruff, South should exit with a trump. West will win and he will be endplayed. A club lead will solve South's problem in that suit. A heart return will yield a ruff and discard. If West had ruffed one of the diamonds, he would have been similarily endplayed.\n\nIf East is the defender with 3 trumps, South should play along similar lines, but he should cash the ♣A early. South must hope that East began with only 2 clubs. When East is eventually thrown in with his trump trick, he will have to concede a ruff and discard.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
